  "articleBody": "When painting interior walls, small shortcuts during preparation can ruin the look of your topcoat. Keep an eye out for these common wall-painting slipups:\n\n- Painting Over Glossy Walls Without Scuffing: New paint cannot bond well to slick surfaces. Lightly sand or use a dedicated bonding primer so your topcoat will not peel off.\n- Forgetting to Prime Patched Drywall: Joint compound absorbs paint differently than surrounding paper, causing visible dull spots known as flashing under normal light.\n- Overloading the Roller: Drenching the roller sleeve causes drips, splatters, and heavy stipple texture instead of an even, smooth finish.\n- Stopping in the Middle of a Wall: Taking a break midway across an open wall causes lap marks where wet paint overlaps dry paint. Always finish a full wall section before stepping away.\n- Judging Color Before Paint Dries: Wet paint looks different from dry paint. Let the coating dry according to the manufacturer guidelines before deciding on color adjustments.\n\nHow to Compare Your Options: Balance the cost of standard builder-grade paint against premium washable coatings, especially in busy hallways and kitchens where durability matters.\n\nFor professional interior wall painting done right the first time, get in touch with Abraham Becerra at HH Remodeling LLC by calling 303-505-0866.",
